And therefore according to the parser are invalid Lojban.

You are taking the fact that they do not parse as evidence that they are=20
  multiple texts.  But formally, if it doesn't parse, it is simply=20
incorrect, even if pieces of it are correct separately.

After all, the following is ungrammatical:

mi klama le zarci do klama

But if one arbitrarily breaks it into two texts either before or after=20
the "do", or before the "le", it is now grammatical.

But the parser won't break it into two texts.  To the parser, it is=20
simply ungrammatical.  The human inputter has to do a break-up,=20
according to informal rules.  One such informal rule is that a new=20
speaker is a new text.  But that is an informal rule of pragmatics, and=20
not part of the definition of Lojban.  The proposals, I think, are=20
proposals to override the informal considerations of pragmatics.  I=20
don't know if this is a good idea unless we codify the rules of Lojban=20
pragmatics.  But I'm willing to consider the possibility.

